  "narration": {
    "title": "The White Sox Own the Week, Two Aces Cool",
    "intro": "The scoreboard doesn't move much day to day, but the past week reshuffled a few things. Chicago's rookies caught fire, a couple of good arms went briefly cold, and the season's biggest bats mostly held serve. Here's who ran hot and who slipped.",
    "hweek_lead": "Tristan Peters had the loudest week of anyone, going 10-for-21 with five homers and nine RBI for a 1.828 OPS, and Seattle's Cal Raleigh matched the five-homer total at 1.558. H\u00e9ctor Rodr\u00edguez chipped in three of his own for Cincinnati.",
    "risers_lead": "Kyle Schwarber keeps padding the longball lead, adding three to reach 40, with Pete Alonso and Luis Garc\u00eda Jr. also up three. Peters is the rookie story here, his OPS climbing 64 points to .821 on the strength of that week.",
    "pfallers_lead": "A few sturdy arms lost a little shine: Chase Burns slipped five points to a form of 71, while Michael McGreevy (down five to 55) and Gavin Williams (down four to 59) also cooled. These are dips, not alarms.",
    "fallers_lead": "Munetaka Murakami went 2-for-22 over six games and gave back 33 points of season OPS, and Tampa Bay's Yandy D\u00edaz cooled too, 4-for-24 for a 21-point drop. Rough weeks for two, nothing more.",
    "colors_lead": "Nobody crossed a tier line this week, which is its own kind of story near the top: Yordan Alvarez still sits alone in elite green at a 1.043 OPS, and Jacob Misiorowski's form of 97 didn't budge. The best stayed the best."
  }
}